<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · 1781 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Gaudeamus igitur</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin student song, sung at university graduations, that tells young people to enjoy life while they can.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; the current Latin text as published and altered by Christian Wilhelm Kindleben</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>European university students</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Halle, Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>current text published 1781; roots in a Latin manuscript of 1287</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics anonymous, current text edited by Christian Wilhelm Kindleben (published 1781))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudeamus-igitur">Gaudeamus igitur</a>&quot; (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for &quot;So let us rejoice&quot;), also called &quot;De brevitate vitae&quot; (&quot;On the Shortness of Life&quot;) or simply &quot;Gaudeamus&quot;, is an academic commercium song, a song of student gatherings, sung across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its claimed origin is a Latin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1287 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheque-nationale-de-france">Bibliothèque nationale de France</a>: a poem in it has two verses close to two of the later song's, though neither the first verse nor the words of the title appear, and its music has nothing to do with the present tune. A German translation of these verses was made in about 1717 and published in 1730 without music. A Latin version in a handwritten student songbook of between 1723 and 1750, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> State Library, differs considerably from the text sung today.</p><p>The modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> words were published in Halle in 1781, with a German translation, in Christian Wilhelm Kindleben's Studentenlieder (&quot;Students' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a>&quot;), and Kindleben admitted that he had changed the text considerably. The melody first appeared in print in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1782, in Lieder für Freunde der Geselligen Freude, with Kindleben's German words, but it was evidently well known before. The Latin text and the present tune were probably first printed together in Ignaz Walter's operatic setting of Goethe's Faust in 1797.</p><p>The song was long a beer-drinking song at many universities. It praises the disorder of student life while keeping in view that everyone will die, in the tradition of carpe diem and memento mori, and it makes ironic jokes about sex and death. Many versions soften the text for public ceremonies, while students in private have sung ruder words. Over the centuries this has produced many slightly different versions.</p><p>Composers took up the tune as a sign of student life: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Berlioz</a> in La Damnation de Faust, Strauss in his &quot;Studenten-Polka&quot; for the students' ball at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> Hofburg on 24 February 1862, Suppé in the overture of his operetta Flotte Bursche (1863), Liszt in a paraphrase and a humoresque (1870), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a> in an arrangement for male chorus (1874), and Brahms in the full-orchestra close of his Academic Festival Overture. Romberg used it in the operetta The Student Prince, set at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>.</p><p>Today it is sung mainly at graduation ceremonies, even though it is a light-hearted song that pokes fun at university life. It is the official song of many schools, colleges, universities and student societies, and the official anthem of the International University Sports Federation. In the United Kingdom it is sometimes called &quot;The Gaudie&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A student song of the shortness of life: since youth passes and death comes to all, the singers resolve to enjoy the time they have. It then turns to toasts in praise of the university, its teachers, its students and the city, mixing jokes with a real awareness of mortality.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gaudeamus igitur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us rejoice, then,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Iuvenes dum sumus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>While we are young;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Post molestam senectutem :,:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>After troublesome old age :,:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Nos habebit humus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The earth will have us.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ubi sunt, qui ante nos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where are those who before us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">In mundo fuere,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Were in the world?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vadite ad superos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Go to those above,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Transite ad inferos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cross over to those below,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ubi jam fuere.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where they already were.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vita nostra brevis est,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our life is short,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Brevi finietur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shortly it will be ended;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Venit mors velociter,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Death comes swiftly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Rapit nos atrociter,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Snatches us cruelly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Nemini parcetur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No one will be spared.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vivat Academia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live the Academy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vivant Professores,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live the professors,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vivat membrum quodlibet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live each member,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vivant membra quaelibet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live all the members,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Semper sint in flore!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May they always be in flower!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vivant omnes virgines</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live all maidens,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Faciles, formosae</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Easy-going, beautiful;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vivant et mulieres :,:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live the women too :,:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Bonae, laboriosae.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Good, hard-working.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vivat et respublica,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live the state too,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et qui illam regit,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And he who rules it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vivat nostra civitas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live our city,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Mecaenatum caritas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The kindness of patrons,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quae nos hic protegit.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Which protects us here.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Pereat tristitia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Perish sadness,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Pereant osores,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Perish the haters,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Pereat diabolus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Perish the devil,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quivis Antiburschius,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Any anti-Bursch whatever,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Atque irrisores.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And the mockers.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional student song (C. W. Kindleben's version, 1781). Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Gaudeamus_Igitur" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>lines 1.3 and 5.3: ':,:' is the original's repeat sign (the line is sung twice) and is kept as it stands.</li><li>lines 2.3-2.5: the sense is disputed. As written, 'Vadite' and 'Transite' are imperatives ('Go ... cross over'): to find the dead you must go to the heavens or the underworld, where they already are. Other versions read 'Abiere ad superos, transiere ad inferos' ('they have gone up ... gone down'), which this text does not have.</li><li>line 2.5: 'Ubi jam fuere' is literally 'where they already were', i.e. where they have long since been.</li><li>lines 4.3-4.4: 'membrum' is a member of the university (student or teacher), not a part of the body.</li><li>line 5.2: 'faciles' means easy, approachable, good-natured; it can also carry the sense 'compliant'.</li><li>line 6.4: 'Mecaenatum' (Maecenatum): of patrons, after Maecenas, Augustus's patron of poets; 'caritas' here is goodwill or generosity.</li><li>line 7.2: 'osores' are haters (of the students).</li><li>line 7.4: 'Antiburschius' is a student-Latin coinage: an enemy of the Burschen, the German students and their fraternities.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
